{
  "source": "Prompts/critical-thinking-reroll-1.mp4",
  "duration": 208.283,
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 6.8,
      "text": "Look at this equation, baseline knowledge provides context to recognize when statements contradict established facts."
    },
    {
      "start": 7.64,
      "end": 13.14,
      "text": "The second half is critical thinking, questioning claims, examining evidence, and considering alternatives."
    },
    {
      "start": 14.2,
      "end": 17.56,
      "text": "Combining knowledge with analysis creates better questions and decisions,"
    },
    {
      "start": 18.1,
      "end": 21.44,
      "text": "building the habit of asking what's required for a claim to be valid."
    },
    {
      "start": 22.22,
      "end": 26.4,
      "text": "In 2015, global news outlets reported a new scientific discovery."
    },
    {
      "start": 26.4,
      "end": 30.88,
      "text": "Major front page headlines announced that eating chocolate could help people lose weight."
    },
    {
      "start": 31.56,
      "end": 34.72,
      "text": "The message was simple and highly shareable, slim by chocolate."
    },
    {
      "start": 35.46,
      "end": 40.8,
      "text": "When an audience sees a headline that offers a desirable result, their reactions generally take one of two directions."
    },
    {
      "start": 41.56,
      "end": 44.28,
      "text": "The first reaction is to accept the information at face value."
    },
    {
      "start": 44.78,
      "end": 48.28,
      "text": "It sounds appealing, it's easy to believe, and it requires no further investigation."
    },
    {
      "start": 49.12,
      "end": 51.0,
      "text": "The second reaction involves an immediate pause."
    },
    {
      "start": 51.0,
      "end": 57.72,
      "text": "Instead of accepting the claim, the reader asks what data or methodology is sitting behind the headline."
    },
    {
      "start": 58.44,
      "end": 61.28,
      "text": "This pause is the most important step in the process."
    },
    {
      "start": 62.08,
      "end": 67.86,
      "text": "You must interrogate a claim most aggressively when it aligns perfectly with what you already want to believe."
    },
    {
      "start": 68.56,
      "end": 74.92,
      "text": "While the chocolate study was technically real, the researchers intentionally designed it to be flimsy and unreliable."
    },
    {
      "start": 75.86,
      "end": 78.64,
      "text": "This diagram illustrates the first major problem."
    },
    {
      "start": 79.46,
      "end": 86.44,
      "text": "The entire study relied on a sample size of just 15 participants, far too small to represent a general population."
    },
    {
      "start": 87.34,
      "end": 93.32,
      "text": "Then, the researchers measured 18 different unrelated variables for each person in that small group."
    },
    {
      "start": 93.84,
      "end": 95.64,
      "text": "This creates a statistical trap."
    },
    {
      "start": 96.36,
      "end": 99.94,
      "text": "If you track 18 different variables across 15 people,"
    },
    {
      "start": 99.94,
      "end": 105.76,
      "text": "the laws of probability dictate that at least one random fluctuation will look like a significant discovery."
    },
    {
      "start": 106.62,
      "end": 109.52,
      "text": "In this instance, that random coincidence was weight loss."
    },
    {
      "start": 110.3,
      "end": 112.68,
      "text": "The research institute was actually just a website."
    },
    {
      "start": 113.5,
      "end": 116.68,
      "text": "The study was a sting operation, conducted by a journalist,"
    },
    {
      "start": 117.26,
      "end": 123.74,
      "text": "to prove that a sensational headline based on poor science would spread across the globe before anyone checked the facts."
    },
    {
      "start": 124.34,
      "end": 127.7,
      "text": "This case demonstrates the core requirement of critical thinking."
    },
    {
      "start": 127.7,
      "end": 131.94,
      "text": "The discipline to investigate the methodology and the source of a claim,"
    },
    {
      "start": 132.56,
      "end": 135.64,
      "text": "particularly when the data is something you find personally appealing."
    },
    {
      "start": 136.64,
      "end": 139.5,
      "text": "You can sharpen this discipline by building five specific habits."
    },
    {
      "start": 140.36,
      "end": 143.78,
      "text": "Each habit is a question you should ask whenever you encounter new information."
    },
    {
      "start": 144.64,
      "end": 146.7,
      "text": "Habit one, is it actually right?"
    },
    {
      "start": 147.36,
      "end": 151.58,
      "text": "Check the evidence supporting the claim and ensure it logically leads to the stated conclusion."
    },
    {
      "start": 152.68,
      "end": 154.66,
      "text": "Habit two, do I know enough to judge?"
    },
    {
      "start": 154.66,
      "end": 160.2,
      "text": "Be honest about the limits of your own knowledge and identify the concepts you still need to understand."
    },
    {
      "start": 160.88,
      "end": 162.46,
      "text": "Habit three, what's missing?"
    },
    {
      "start": 163.18,
      "end": 167.78,
      "text": "Actively look for omitted data points or alternative explanations that might change the context."
    },
    {
      "start": 168.72,
      "end": 170.4,
      "text": "Habit four, why am I convinced?"
    },
    {
      "start": 171.02,
      "end": 176.88,
      "text": "Determine if you are persuaded by the evidence itself or by a confident tone and your own internal biases."
    },
    {
      "start": 178.08,
      "end": 179.94,
      "text": "Habit five, what's my call?"
    },
    {
      "start": 180.5,
      "end": 186.02,
      "text": "Decide what to believe based on the available facts, but remain open to changing your mind as you learn more."
    },
    {
      "start": 186.84,
      "end": 189.08,
      "text": "These questions are vital when working with AI."
    },
    {
      "start": 189.86,
      "end": 195.52,
      "text": "Large language models can help you find a flaw in your thinking, or they can confidently repeat your errors back to you."
    },
    {
      "start": 196.16,
      "end": 199.72,
      "text": "The responsibility for judging the output rests entirely with the user."
    },
    {
      "start": 200.62,
      "end": 202.4,
      "text": "AI amplifies whatever you bring to it."
    },
    {
      "start": 203.06,
      "end": 204.54,
      "text": "Good thinking in? Sharper output."
    }
  ],
